      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a more detailed explanation of mitosis, broken down into clear phases:\n\nMitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two identical daughter cells. To do this, the cell must replicate its DNA and prepare for cell division. This is achieved through a series of phases: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase.\n\nIn **prophase**, the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, and the nuclear envelope breaks down. The spindle fibers, which will later form the mitotic spindle, begin to form at the center of the cell. The centrioles, which are similar to tiny movie projectors, move to opposite ends of the cell and release the microtubules of the spindle fibers.\n\nAs the cell enters **metaphase**, the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers. Each chromosome has attached to a specific microtubule, forming bonds called kinetochore attachments. This alignment is crucial for ensuring that each daughter cell receives the correct chromosomes during division.\n\nIn **anaphase**, the sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles of the cell. The microtubules of the spindle fibers contract and release their grip, allowing the",
      "source": "target",
